#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:5
#: f9f0a584af5e4b4fa3924079195e2b85
msgid "RunCam Camera Support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:8
#: 9908b73134b14551acaceee4bb7040aa
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:9
#: f29f79526d2f40889094074a40e80bde
msgid "RunCam devices support the `Runcam Device Protocol <https://support.runcam.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014537794-RunCam-Device-Protocol>`_ which is the serial communication protocol initially developed for the RunCam Split and analog cameras."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:11
#: b1ca1b536e644a47a21c0e6ac835575c
msgid "The protocol itself supports not only full camera OSD menu navigation but also triggering recording on split style cameras as well as communicating the available SD-card space, syncing the current date, writing to the OSD and turning the camera or WiFi function (if available) on and off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:13
#: da33778c77984844b977ce2fdac68988
msgid "Not all features of the protocol are currently supported in ArduPilot and not all features of the protocol are supported on all cameras. In particular it is important to distinguish between cameras that support the \"5-button protocol\" (generally camera -only devices such as the RunCam Racer) and cameras that support the \"2-button protocol\" (generally split-style cameras). These two protocols originated from the 5-key external OSD keyboard and 2 buttons present on split-style cameras. Equally important is to understand that the RunCam device protocol is almost a direct mapping from the hardware - so the way that you would physically press a button is the way that the protocol is invoked. The only caveat here is that the 5-button protocol recognizes keys being released, whereas the 2-button protocol does not, leading to some limitations in the way that menus and options can be navigated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:15
#: a093c701424d4c329af2e5587a93a051
msgid "Lately, similar to TBS Smartaudio and IRC Tramp, other companies (for instance Caddx) have started to adopt support for the protocol in their cameras. Currently supported features in ArduPilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:18
#: 8df8f20e873c4098af6e0ad036d037e9
msgid "Full camera OSD control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:19
#: 1ef63fc39dc441aba67a4b033709e5f5
msgid "Starting/Stopping recording on split style cameras"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:22
#: 25a8b026164e42beb17f4074691390cb
msgid "Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:24
#: e33749ebd58a45b29f1185d05e984445
msgid "One free non-inverted UART on the FC is required (TX and RX). Connect TX to RX, RX to TX and GND to GND. Pinouts for serial ports on The Cube can be found :ref:`here <common-pixhawk-serial-mapping>`. Check the :ref:`board documentation<common-autopilots>` for other boards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:30
#: 4ee47a1675e44b55b3ca6865ef7c6c22
msgid "On some camera-only devices the pinout is labeled such that TX should be connected to TX and RX to RX. If your RunCam device is not recognized try reversing the signal wires. Not all cameras support a UART ground pin - they expect to get ground from the power supply."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:32
#: 72afa8927d1b4f499dd1979cb1d56c6c
msgid "The camera must be in UART control mode. On split-style cameras this should be the default. On camera-only devices you will need to follow the procedure for putting the device in UART control mode. Consult your RunCam manual for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:34
#: 9f2c1f53a7cb475da0ee09ac53fdea90
msgid "Note that so-called \"Joystick control\" is not supported and some devices only support joystick control, so be careful when selecting the RunCam model you are going to use. The following devices have been tested successfully - RunCam Split 3s Micro, RunCam Split 2s, RunCam Racer 3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:36
#: 3828c2e75a9b489ca5533fd92142204f
msgid "ArduPilot supports up to two cameras. For the rest of this article, it is assumed that the RunCam is setup as the first camera."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:38
#: af405f3be30a4a5cb03267e709acceaa
msgid "Set :ref:`CAM1_TYPE<CAM1_TYPE>` = \"8\" (RunCam)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:40
#: 9254e70fd16a48c79ca44e07235a94b5
msgid "In order to recognize the RunCam serial protocol on the UART, set the UART serial protocol to 26. So for instance for serial port 5, set :ref:`SERIAL5_PROTOCOL <SERIAL5_PROTOCOL>` = \"26\" (RunCam protocol). The correlation between serial port numbering and UART physical ports for your autopilot should be documented in its description page linked :ref:`here <common-autopilots>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:42
#: ff67dba2575f42aaadeb8622633378cf
msgid "When the camera is connected correctly and the serial port configured correctly you will see a message similar to the following at boot: ``APM: RunCam initialized, features 0x0077, 2-key OSD`` otherwise you will see ``APM: RunCam device not found``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:44
#: 3d8b387517b24bbe946322bf603e2ed0
msgid "The camera must be powered and fully booted in order to accept commands. This can take some time and the delay between the autopilot booting and the camera being probed can be controlled through :ref:`CAM1_RC_BTN_DELY<CAM1_RC_BTN_DELY>`. The camera will be probed repeatedly until :ref:`CAM1_RC_BTN_DELY<CAM1_RC_BTN_DELY>` elapses, but once that has expired the camera will not be probed again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:46
#: 1a5c3ce16736453e9fa41ba415d0d044
msgid "Some split-style cameras support three modes - video standby, video start and picture mode. It is not possible to tell from the RunCam device protocol whether this is the type of camera in use and so this must be configured."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:47
#: f8854c3a8d394ec0a89cd871c324833c
msgid "Set :ref:`CAM1_RC_TYPE<CAM1_RC_TYPE>` = \"2\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:49
#: 163a1b8a3ead41dc9a98ca6e8a2c44a0
msgid "Most other cameras should be fine with this value, however some of the later cameras have a bug where video start/stop protocol commands are not properly supported and a workaround has to be used. Cameras known to have this problem are the RunCam Split4k and require setting :ref:`CAM1_RC_TYPE<CAM1_RC_TYPE>` = \"3\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:50
#: d4cea501f2d84b10b7559cdbf734629b
msgid "In addition to this problem RunCam Hybrid cameras have a QR code mode which can also cause problems for recording control. For RunCam Hybrids set :ref:`CAM1_RC_TYPE<CAM1_RC_TYPE>` = \"4\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:52
#: 9d0eb3801e0c4e008f9ea73e195dd14d
msgid "For RunCam2 4K camera, set :ref:`CAM1_RC_TYPE<CAM1_RC_TYPE>` = \"5\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:54
#: 96c26c73853e41ecabde1aa50fd3c57b
msgid ":ref:`CAM1_RC_MDE_DELY <CAM1_RC_MDE_DELY>` is the time it takes for the a RunCam mode button press to be activated in ms. If a mode change first requires a video recording change then double this value is used. If this is too short then commands can get out of sync."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:57
#: 4a28e0d99e5440ee91f33feeae234665
msgid "RunCam is bringing out new cameras all the time and seem to change the video controls with almost every camera, so if you are having issues - particularly with video control - try one of the other camera types for :ref:`CAM1_RC_TYPE<CAM1_RC_TYPE>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:59
#: 7f3c5ee702024af893008c76d4f79d46
msgid "In order to control start and stop of video recording from a radio transmitter set a free rc channel to ``RCx_OPTION`` = 78 to enable triggering from that RC input channel. If the switch is in the low position then video recording is stopped, if in the high position then video recording is started."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:61
#: 92fbcacf52754739a80163118cb71276
msgid "In order to control entering the OSD menu set a free rc channel to ``RCx_OPTION`` = 79. If the switch is in the low position then the OSD menu is exited, if in the high position then the OSD menu is entered. The OSD menu can also be entered by default through rolling hard right while disarmed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:63
#: 7fb345ae25944abfbe107415d84cf55f
msgid "The available features of the attached RunCam device are queried by the driver but can be disabled by setting up the :ref:`CAM1_RC_FEATURES<CAM1_RC_FEATURES>` bitmask with the desired features to enable (assuming the camera model actually supports them)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:66
#: 95891522a9e346cf8e8caefd5fcf4b8f
msgid "OSD Menu Control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:67
#: dbc08f6681ee4e51a896ec358bea0a3a
msgid "A big part of the RunCam feature is support for the OSD menu. The OSD menu allows various properties of a connected camera to be controlled via RC stick gestures. The easiest way to enter the OSD menu is to push the stick for Roll hard right. This gesture is enabled by default but can be changed by setting :ref:`CAM1_RC_CONTROL<CAM1_RC_CONTROL>`. Once in the menu, the Pitch stick will allow you to navigated between menu items and Roll right will usually allow you to select items or navigate into sub-menus. The OSD menu can only be entered while disarmed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:69
#: 66ccd5bb999449f8b86c4adfe90c4144
msgid "The transmitter sticks can be used to control the OSD menu as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:75
#: 9023e641a1144641973110e33d09415c
msgid "Split-style cameras differ significantly from regular RunCams in terms of what gestures are allowed. On a RunCam with UART control the stick gestures match the operation of the remote keyboard that can be used with the camera. The roll and pitch stick gestures correspond to up, down, left and right on the keyboard and yaw corresponds to enter. On a Split-style camera pitch up and down have the same effect of cycling through menu items and enter is roll right. Although every effort has been made to make the user interfaces of the two-camera styles consistent, deficiencies in the RunCam remote protocol make this challenging. The two most obvious differences are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:77
#: 7dc6868e54114a92a42bad244da973e7
msgid "Split-style cameras allow exit from a sub-menu through the left gesture whereas regular RunCams' require the exit menu option to be selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:78
#: 8a5c58367d3942be94f4718b72091c1f
msgid "Split-style cameras allow exit from the OSD through the left gesture whereas regular RunCams' require the use of the configured exit OSD command (either a TX switch or yaw left)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:81
#: 708cb1d7ed67458495d5841a970f1ccd
msgid "OSD Menu and OSD Interaction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-camera-runcam.rst:83
#: 36d051a69d36477cb92444adccb5f4d6
msgid "One of the features of the ArduPilot implementation of OSD menu control is that the autopilot will switch off the regular flight OSD when the camera OSD is entered. It will also prevent arming whilst in OSD mode. When the OSD menu is exited the regular flight OSD will be re-enabled and arming allowed."
msgstr ""
